# Lightning of Jealousy! Thunder of Abuse! A Downpour of Invective!
Tamil title: மாச்சரிய மின்னல்! ஏசல் இடி! வசை மாறி!

## The Eluru Incident: A Lesson

“I am dissolving the meeting!”

“No! No! Do not dissolve it!”

“Do you want it dissolved? Take a vote! A vote!”

“The disorder has become excessive. The meeting can no longer continue, so I dissolve it.”

“Shame! Shame! Shame!”

The chair dissolves the meeting. Those present roar that his order has no validity. Their fury emerges in many words such as “Shame!” and “Disgrace!” The existing confusion increases, the gathering reaches a very ugly condition, and finally disperses.

* * *

That is ordinary enough, is it not? This is how supporters of one party break up another party's meeting. Creating disorder at meetings has become a great art in our public life. What can be done? The existence of many parties supposedly produces this trouble.

Many sweet-tongued advisers preach that parties and counter-parties should not exist if we wish to prevent mental jealousy, quarrelling and the disfigurement of public life. Some journals have begun writing openly and shamelessly that there should be one party to govern but no opposition to discover, state and correct defects in its principles or programme.

Is not government without opposition called dictatorship, Fascism and Nazism? The intellectual world has condemned it, and experience has shown that it brings only destruction. Asked how they dare say no other party should exist, some lower their tone and request a monopoly for at least a few years, like a caught thief begging protection in a weeping voice after being beaten.

Others refuse even then to reduce their noise. Grinding their teeth, they demand: “Do you know who I am? Do you know my heroic valour?” Fortunately, the number of such people is small and continues to decline.

Noise and foolish schemes do not increase support for a ruling party; they reduce it. Yet the natural joy of obtaining power can become conceit. Reason then grows clouded, and dull minds begin imagining that the world will bow at their feet if their anger rises.

If even intellectuals can be driven by such conceit, what need is there to speak of ordinary people? During a cyclone, great trees may lose balance and fall, while rubbish sometimes lands upon the top of a tower. Intelligent people do not mock the fallen tree as weak and praise the rubbish atop the tower as powerful. Only poor minds think that way.

In political life too, people who obtain authority may imagine it permanent or believe it came entirely through their own merit. This does not happen to those who read and remember history, but to those elevated merely because they happened to stand where the wind of victory blew. Some journals forget their duty to open such people's intellectual eyes and instead feed their conceit.

Those who do not know that conceit is a hidden poison consume it. Dictatorship is customarily born in this environment. We ask the public to recognize the immense harm done by journals that say no party may exist against the ruling one.

Minister Dr Subbarayan, understanding that the intellectual world would condemn this tendency, explained the reasons for several restrictions by saying, “We have no intention of banning any political party.” Let the journals urging the ruling party toward dictatorship heed the minister's counsel.

In an age when democratic doctrine is accepted as the anchor of civilized politics, if some people still imagine that no opposition should exist, we can call it only the Berlin disease. Dr Subbarayan's refusal to fall into its grip deserves praise.

Suppose instead that the doctor too becomes ill, and the ruling party surrenders to incense-burning journals, bans all parties or makes it impossible for them to function. Suppose every other party disappears. What will happen? The scene presented at the beginning explains the likely result.

The opening report describes a meeting in which disorder and strife grew, the chair's command collided with the audience's cries, and confusion intensified. This disorder did not result from a collision between one political party and its opposition. It arose from disagreement within a single party: a family quarrel.

Anyone who says parties must not engage in conflict and therefore no opposition should exist would be supremely foolish to imagine that dissolving parties dissolves differences of opinion or creates a wasteland in the public mind where disagreement can never sprout.

Differences arise inside a single party too. The resulting condition will not possess the dignity created by democratic parties. It will become a jungle of shouting. There will be no orderly debate, explanation or propagation of principles. Committee meetings will contain noise, substitution of people and names, members singing sacred hymns about one another's “auspicious virtues,” and measurements of who is the true thief. The opening report shows what conditions then become. Read it once more.

During elections one party may imagine it can weaken another's propaganda by disturbing its meetings. The public may suffer and quarrels may occur through the campaign warfare of both sides. Lovers of peace say this should not happen.

Some use that as a pretext to spread Nazism by claiming only one party should exist. Even if, as they desire, every party were dissolved and Congress alone remained, this report gives an example of the scenes the country would still witness.

The West Godavari Congress Committee meeting began at two on Sunday afternoon. On Monday evening it ended amid tremendous disorder. Declaring that he could not control the situation, the chairman dissolved it.

A committee meeting with so much disorder! It lasted thirty hours, and throughout that time there was conflict, confusion, abuse and thunder: lightning of jealousy and a downpour of invective.

The thirty-hour meeting did not remain in one place. The festival of quarrelling shifted its camp among three sites in Eluru: the Merchants' Association grounds, Gandhi School and the committee building. There was disorder at all three.

The committee had assembled merely to discuss income and expenditure accounts and the annual report, not to explain principles or frame a programme. It had failed to meet for fifteen months, and this was the result when it finally did. Why did the confusion occur? What turned the committee meeting into a battlefield?

Had members of the Godavari Communist Party broken up a Godavari District Congress public meeting, one could say such conflicts arose because separate Congress and Communist parties existed. But this conflict occurred within the Congress Committee itself.

They met in one place and spoke for hours until the quarrel deepened. They sought another place, where the conflict followed them. They moved to a third and tried again, but confusion arose there too. After thirty hours, the committee chairman sang, “I can bear this suffering no longer,” and dissolved the meeting while many cried “Shame!” Such was the distinguished character of this committee session.

The “wise men” who say no party other than Congress should show its head in the country must observe the Eluru incident. What happens when parties are dissolved? Eluru appears everywhere.

One issue debated by the Congress committee was whether it had served the farmers of the district.

Comrade S. Satyanarayana Raju charged that the District Congress Committee had made no effort in zamindari areas to remedy cultivators' grievances, improve their condition and win their confidence. Other members denied the charge. He answered; some condemned his conduct, while others supported his speech. Debate expanded, a storm of words blew, and confusion danced.

Committee chairman Comrade Surya Narayana became distressed. They changed locations, but the trouble followed. The disorder strengthened, and the chairman dissolved the meeting.

When a district Congress committee debated whether West Godavari Congress had benefited cultivators, it produced thirty hours of disorder and ended in chaos. If discussion of one particular problem within Congress itself caused this, then if differences over many problems are concealed and everyone is forced into one camp, will not Eluru occur everywhere every day?

Friends who say no party but Congress should exist must ask whether that would be good or civilized. We do not believe they possess magical power to turn people into logs of wood. It is not only unavoidable that people form opinions about the many problems before them; that quality should be honoured and cultivated.

When parties form around issues, they can campaign upon those issues and gather public support. The democratic principle flourishes when the position receiving majority support becomes the country's policy. If that system is destroyed, committee meetings throughout the country will become forests of conflict like Eluru, and neither peace nor order will prevail.

Many people may think, “Why suffer in other parties? Let us enter Congress itself.” Once they enter, how will the resulting conflict be prevented, and who will prevent it?

If Communists said Congress members in Eluru had not helped zamindari tenants, Congress could dismiss it as “Moscow talk” that the people of India would never believe, condemn those Communists as traitors to the August movement and conceal the issue.

That route is no longer available. Those charging that the Congress committee failed to help farmers are themselves Congress members. Those crying “No, no!” in denial are Congress members too.

The country would see countless such “novelties wearing the sacred thread” if the claim that no party besides Congress should exist became policy.

After reflecting upon Eluru, friends whose victory has made them forget wisdom must understand that democratic political parties, which express disagreements methodically, orderly and honestly, are a sign of progress.

They must abandon the wish for a vehicle without brakes and allow the democratic system to grow: a ruling party and an alternative party bearing the political responsibility of watching its course.

Otherwise, instead of separate parties, factions will arise within the party; conflict will arise inside committees; and a jungle system of counting how many people belong to this man or that one will dominate and make politics and public life uncivilized.

The slogan “Our party members are sacrificers, heroes and wise men” cannot remain useful for long. When conflict arises within the party itself, even those words lose meaning:

“I went to prison three times.”

“Yes, and the fourth time you begged forgiveness and ran out. I know.”

“Unlike you, I did not join the party for a contract.”

“A very worthy man indeed! Shut your mouth. Did you not wrap yourself in khadi to obtain a job for your son-in-law?”

“Speak respectfully!”

“You speak humbly!”

“Be careful!”

“Watch yourself!”

“Get lost, black-marketeer!”

“Shut your mouth, crooked hanger-on!”

“I shall finish you and then attend to other work.”

“A mighty tiger indeed! Are Congress artists not in your hands?”

“Look, if I do not expel you from Congress...”

“I shall dissolve this very committee and move on.”

“I shall telegraph Patel...”

“Do whatever you can. I shall meet Nehru directly.”

Such “conversations” and Eluru-like “incidents” will become abundant under the one-party government our friends propose. Does the country desire this? Should people of good sense desire it?

*Dravida Nadu*, 21 March 1948

---NOTES---
`மாச்சரியம்` means jealousy, envy or rancour. The title extends a storm metaphor through lightning, thunder and rain. `எல்லூர்` is Eluru in the then Godavari region; the article later specifies the West Godavari Congress Committee.

The discussion responds to calls for Congress to enjoy a monopoly and for opposition parties to disappear. `பெர்லின் நோய்` (“Berlin disease”) refers to Nazi one-party dictatorship. Dr P. Subbarayan is cited as rejecting a general ban on political parties.

The OCR of Comrade Satyanarayana Raju's initials and credentials is badly damaged (`4.` and `14.1...1.`), while chairman Surya Narayana's name appears as `குரியநாராயணா`. These are normalized only in the English from context; the Tamil source remains unchanged. `பூணாரம் பூண்ட புதுமைகள்` uses the sacred thread satirically.

The final invented quarrel deliberately imitates factional invective. One line OCRed as `காங்கிரஸ் கலைவர்கள்` may mean Congress leaders rather than artists; the contextual taunt is retained conservatively. Catalogue number `26.` is not part of the title.
